	regionKind = "zone",
	kind = "region",
	sections = {
		{
			title = "Summary",
			text = [=[Blackrock Mountain is a volcano that should not exist. It rises black and smoking between the {link:searing-gorge}Searing Gorge{/link} to the north and the {link:burning-steppes}Burning Steppes{/link} to the south, and its summit is hidden in a pall of ash that never lifts. It was not raised by the slow work of the world. It was born in a single day of fire, when a dwarven sorcerer reached for a power he could not master and called up a lord of flame from beneath the earth.

Within its walls the mountain is hollow. A vast caldera opens at its heart, floored with molten rock, and across it hang chains so great that their links are walked like bridges. Around that burning pit the builders of the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} have carved halls, stairs, forges and gates on a scale no other people of the {link:eastern-kingdoms}Eastern Kingdoms{/link} has ever matched. Down below lies their capital. High above rises the fortress of the Spire. Deeper still, where even the Dark Irons walk in fear, the fire that made the mountain still burns.

No banner of the {link:alliance}Alliance{/link} or the {link:horde}Horde{/link} flies here. The mountain belongs to its masters, and its masters are at war with one another.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Origins",
			text = [=[The land where the mountain now stands was once green. In ancient days, when all the {link:dwarves}dwarves{/link} of Khaz Modan still answered to a single high king in {link:ironforge}Ironforge{/link}, the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Iron clan{/link} was one of three great clans beneath his throne. When the high king died, the Bronzebeards, the Wildhammers and the Dark Irons fought for his crown. The Bronzebeards held Ironforge. The Dark Irons, led by the sorcerer-thane {link:ruins-of-thaurissan}Thaurissan{/link}, went south into the mountains and raised a city of their own, which bore his name.

Some two hundred and thirty years before the {link:dark-portal}Dark Portal{/link} opened, the Dark Irons struck back. Thaurissan marched on Ironforge, while his wife, the sorceress Modgud, marched on the Wildhammer seat of {link:grim-batol}Grim Batol{/link}. Both assaults failed. Modgud fell at Grim Batol. Thaurissan was thrown back from the gates of Ironforge. The armies of the Bronzebeard king Madoran and the Wildhammer king Khardros then turned south together to end the Dark Iron threat for good.

Thaurissan did not wait for them. Grieving and desperate, he sought to draw a weapon out of the burning depths of the world, and he wove the greatest spell of his life. What answered him was not a weapon but a master. Ragnaros the Firelord, lord of the fire {link:elements}elementals{/link}, was torn into the world through the sorcerer's working. His coming shattered the mountains for leagues around. The city of Thaurissan was swallowed. The land to the south burned to cinders, and the land to the north split open into a smoking chasm. Madoran and Khardros halted their armies and went home, for there was nothing left to fight that they could hope to defeat.

Where the Dark Iron city had stood, a volcano rose. The Firelord withdrew into its molten heart, and the Dark Irons who survived became his slaves.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "The Present Day",
			text = [=[The mountain is a battlefield with two thrones and no truce.

Below, in {red:blackrock-depths}Blackrock Depths{/red}, the {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} have built Shadowforge City, their capital, and there {red:emperor-dagran-thaurissan}Emperor Dagran Thaurissan{/red}, heir of the sorcerer-thane, rules in the Firelord's name. Beneath even his halls lies the {red:molten-core}Molten Core{/red}, where {red:ragnaros}Ragnaros{/red} waits in the fire, served by his elemental lieutenants. The Dark Irons forge weapons and golems without rest, and their war parties go up into {link:searing-gorge}the Searing Gorge{/link}, the {link:badlands}Badlands{/link} and beyond.

Above, in {red:lower-blackrock-spire}Blackrock Spire{/red}, the orcs of the {link:blackrock-clan}Blackrock Clan{/link} hold the heights. They have gathered beneath {red:warchief-rend-blackhand}Warchief Rend Blackhand{/red}, son of the old Warchief Blackhand, who calls his following a {link:horde}Horde{/link} and himself its Warchief, in open defiance of {link:thrall}Thrall{/link}. {link:ogres}Ogres{/link} and trolls fight beside them. At the very summit, in {red:blackwing-lair}Blackwing Lair{/red}, dwells the black dragon {red:nefarian}Nefarian{/red}, son of {link:deathwing}Deathwing{/link}, and the orcs of the Spire are his to command.

Between the two thrones lies the open caldera, and both sides guard their gates. King {link:magni-bronzebeard}Magni Bronzebeard{/link} believes that his daughter, {red:princess-moira-bronzebeard}Moira{/red}, is held somewhere in Shadowforge City. The Horde has sent its hunters against Rend. Scholars, mages and treasure seekers of every nation gather at the mountain's doors, and few of them come back the way they went in.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Inhabitants",
			text = [=[• {b}The Dark Iron dwarves.{/b} Masters of fire, stone and dark craft. They hold the depths and guard the ways down to their city.
• {b}The fire elementals.{/b} Servants of the Firelord, they walk the quarries and forges below as openly as the dwarves who labor there.
• {b}The {link:blackrock-clan}Blackrock orcs{/link}.{/b} The oldest of the war clans, still keeping the ways of the old {link:horde}Horde{/link}. They hold the Spire and the roads that climb to it.
• {b}The ogres and trolls of the Spire.{/b} They serve Rend's cause as allies and as muscle.
• {b}The black dragonflight.{/b} Nefarian's brood keeps the summit, and his whelps and drakonids are bred in its halls.
• {b}The dead.{/b} Not every Dark Iron who built the mountain has left it. At least one of its old masters still keeps watch over the city he raised.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "History",
			text = [=[• {b}The War of the Three Hammers.{/b} The {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} fought the Bronzebeards and the Wildhammers for the rule of all dwarves, and lost.
• {b}The Coming of the Firelord.{/b} {link:ruins-of-thaurissan}Thaurissan{/link}'s summoning of Ragnaros raised the mountain, created {link:searing-gorge}the Searing Gorge{/link} and the {link:burning-steppes}Burning Steppes{/link}, and bound the Dark Iron nation in servitude.
• {b}The Building of Shadowforge.{/b} Under the Firelord's rule, the Dark Irons carved their new capital into the mountain's heart and raised the Spire above it. The architect {link:franclorn-forgewright}Franclorn Forgewright{/link} designed much of their work.
• {b}The {link:shadow-council}Shadow Council{/link}.{/b} In the days of the {link:first-war}First War{/link}, the warlocks who ruled the orcs from the shadows made their seat in Blackrock Spire. When {link:stormwind-city}Stormwind{/link} fell, {link:orgrim-doomhammer}Orgrim Doomhammer{/link} turned on them there, slew most of them and took {link:gul-dan}Gul'dan{/link} captive.
• {b}The Seat of the {link:horde}Horde{/link}.{/b} In the {link:second-war}Second War{/link}, Doomhammer made the Spire the stronghold of his Horde. The mountain bore the name of his own clan, and the Blackrock made it their domain.
• {b}The Last Battle.{/b} At the end of the Second War, the {link:alliance}Alliance{/link} drove the Horde back to the mountain. At its foot {link:anduin-lothar}Anduin Lothar{/link} fell by Doomhammer's hand. {link:turalyon}Turalyon{/link} took up his sword, rallied the grieving army and broke the Horde. The survivors were taken into the internment camps, and only a remnant of the Blackrock escaped into the mountain.
• {b}The Dark Horde.{/b} In the years that followed, the Blackrock gathered again in the heights under Rend Blackhand, while Nefarian and his brood came to the summit and bent the orcs to his will. The war for the mountain between dragon and Firelord has not ended since.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Lore of the Mountain",
			text = [=[• The name of Blackrock is older than the orcs. Doomhammer is said to have taken it as a good omen that the mountain he claimed bore his clan's name.
• The great chains that hang across the caldera are the only road between the gates of the mountain and the ways down to Shadowforge. To cross them is to walk above a lake of fire.
• Before the gates of the Dark Iron city lies a quarry where fire itself stands guard. The mages of {link:morgan-s-vigil}Morgan's Vigil{/link} have lost more than one of their number there.
• In a passage off the road to the Spire, a dark orb rests upon a pedestal. It bears the print of a hand, and it answers to no one who does not carry its master's command.
• The {link:dark-iron-dwarves}Dark Irons{/link} who broke with their emperor now call themselves the {link:thorium-brotherhood}Thorium Brotherhood{/link}. Their smiths keep a camp at {link:thorium-point}Thorium Point{/link} in {link:searing-gorge}the Searing Gorge{/link}, close enough to the mountain to watch its gates.]=],
		},
	},
})

