	sections = {
		{
			title = "Summary",
			text = [=[Franclorn Forgewright was the chief architect of the Dark Iron nation, and the mountain is full of his work. He gave his name to the stonewrought method of building, and he boasts, not without cause, that half the world has walked across his creations without knowing it. He died a hero of his people. He did not die in peace. His spirit still stands at the tomb they raised for him, at the entrance of the city he planned, and it shows itself only to the dead.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Significance",
			text = [=[Franclorn is one of the few voices from inside the Dark Iron nation who will speak honestly of what it has become. He served his king and his people when the Firelord came, and he chose to stay with them. He watched the corruption of Ragnaros seep into the hearts of an honorable race until it drove them to madness and to evil. He knows the price of that choice, and he does not pretend that it was the right one.

He also holds a secret of the city itself. As chief architect he kept the master key to Shadowforge, and he hid it in a place only he could open.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Deeds",
			text = [=[• He devised the stonewrought method of building, which bears his name.
• By his own account, he raised the Spire and a dwarven city mightier than any before it, when the heart of the mountain was torn out at the Firelord's command.
• He forged the great hammer Ironfel and kept it as his own.
• He designed the monument to himself that stands at the Shrine of {link:ruins-of-thaurissan}Thaurissan{/link}, with a hidden compartment for the master key of the city and an empty grip made for Ironfel alone.
• When the Spire was finished, his will to live among his people failed. He was buried as a hero of the new Dark Iron nation.]=],
